              "asynchronous_ai_overview": false,
              "markdown": "SEC Form 4 transaction codes are `single-letter identifiers found in Column 3 of Table I and Table II that explain changes in company insiders' beneficial ownership` . You can view the official code definitions on the [SEC Ownership Form Codes page](https://www.sec.gov/edgar/searchedgar/ownershipformcodes.html).[[1]](https://www.finrep.ai/blog/sec-form-4-transaction-codes-the-definitive-reference)[[2]](https://www.sec.gov/edgar/searchedgar/ownershipformcodes.html)\n\n- **P** : Open market or private purchase of securities\n- **S** : Open market or private sale of securities\n- **V** : Transaction voluntarily reported earlier than required[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Form_4)\n\nAdditional SEC Form 4 transaction codes categorize transactions under Rule 16b-3, derivative securities, exempt and small acquisitions, and other specific events. For the complete and detailed breakdown of each specific code (such as codes A, D, F, I, M for Rule 16b-3; C, E, H, O, X for derivatives; G, L, W, Z for exempt transactions; and J, K, U for other events), please refer to the official [SEC Ownership Form Codes page](https://www.sec.gov/edgar/searchedgar/ownershipformcodes.html).[[1]](https://www.sec.gov/edgar/searchedgar/ownershipformcodes.html)[[2]](https://www.finrep.ai/blog/sec-form-4-transaction-codes-the-definitive-reference)[[3]](https://www.secdatabase.com/Articles/tabid/42/ArticleID/10/Form-4-Transaction-Code-Definitions.aspx)\n\nIf you are analyzing a specific filing, let me know the **transaction code(s)** you see, and I can explain what the trade typically signals.",
